    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.clique_graph - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.clique_graph.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.clique_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Build the clique graph: one node per maximal clique, linked when cliques\n    overlap.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.clique_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Build the clique graph: one node per maximal clique, linked when cliques\n    overlap.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.clique_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Build the clique graph: one node per maximal clique, linked when cliques\n  overlap.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nBuild the clique graph: one node per maximal clique, linked when cliques overlap.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L34794-L34891){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.clique_graph()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Maximal clique graph](../assets/graphs/clique-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: two triangles A, B, C and C, E, F sharing node C, plus a tail C to D; the three maximal cliques are tinted regions in the accent color, teal and violet, all overlapping at C. Right: the clique graph has one node per clique, labeled {A,B,C}, {C,D} and {C,E,F} in the same colors, and an edge for every pair that shares a node: all three pairs share C, named on each edge, so 3 edges.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nEach maximal clique of this graph becomes a node, and two are joined when the cliques\nshare at least one node. The clique graph shows how a network's tight groups overlap and\nchain together, a coarse view used in community analysis (it underlies clique\npercolation) and in the theory of clique graphs and intersection graphs.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new undirected graph. Each node is labeled with its clique's members, such as\n    `\"{A,B,C}\"`, and stores their node IDs in the attribute `\"clique\"`.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe maximal cliques come from `find_cliques()`, and every pair is tested for overlap,\n$O(k^2 s)$ for $k$ cliques of size up to $s$. Matches NetworkX's `make_max_clique_graph`\n(which numbers the cliques 0, 1, ... instead of labeling them by members).\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~find_cliques`) \n\n:   List the maximal cliques that become its nodes.\n\n[](`~cmty_k_clique`) \n\n:   Communities of adjacent cliques of size k.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nTwo triangles share node `C`, and a tail `C`-`D` hangs off it. All three maximal cliques\nhold `C`, so the clique graph is a triangle:\n\n::: {#6d8cca13 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\"), (\"C\", \"E\"), (\"E\", \"F\"),\n     (\"F\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\")]\n)\ncliques = graph.clique_graph()\n\ncliques.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n['{C,E,F}--{A,B,C}', '{C,E,F}--{C,D}', '{A,B,C}--{C,D}']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### How tight groups connect\n\n`friends()` links 24 members of two clubs in a made-up town, a chess club and a climbing\nclub, who are friends. Its 21 maximal cliques become the nodes of the clique graph, with\nan edge for each pair that shares a member:\n\n::: {#240dfb72 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfriends = ec.friends()\ngroups = friends.clique_graph()\n\ngroups.count_nodes(), groups.count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(21, 35)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nColoring each clique by the clubs of its members shows where the clubs meet. Four cliques\nmix them, and all four hold Hugo and Tara, which joins them in the clique graph too:\n\n::: {#1ab00bc8 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nclub = dict(friends.get_node_df().select(\"id\", \"club\").iter_rows())\n\ndef clubs(members):\n    found = {club[n] for n in members}\n    return found.pop() if len(found) == 1 else \"Both\"\n\nmix = {n: clubs(groups.get_node(n).attributes[\"clique\"])\n       for n in groups.get_node_ids()}\ngroups.set_node_attrs(\"clubs\", mix).plot(\n    color_by=\"clubs\", label_by=\"label\", seed=1, height=480\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d11